      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.048,
            0.418,
            0.065
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8 | Part-II"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.09,
            0.846,
            0.129
          ],
          "content": "Since $q \\geq p ,$ we can see that $r p \\times q$ is bigger than $r q \\times p .$ . This leaves us with a comparison between $q p \\times r$ and $r p \\times q .$ If we expand these, we get"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.133,
            0.469,
            0.152
          ],
          "content": "\\cdot \\quad q p \\times r = ( 1 0 \\times q \\times r ) + ( p \\times r )",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.156,
            0.47,
            0.176
          ],
          "content": "$r p \\times q = ( 1 0 \\times r \\times q ) + ( p \\times q )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.18,
            0.847,
            0.274
          ],
          "content": "Once again, the first term is the same in both expressions. Since $r \\geq q$ the second term of the first expression is larger, so the largest product is $q p   \\times   r .$ . This matches our earlier observation that the largest digit should be the multiplier and the other two digits should be arranged in decreasing order to form the multiplicand."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.282,
            0.295,
            0.304
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.305,
            0.847,
            0.345
          ],
          "content": "1. Fill the digits 1, 3, and 7 in $\\bigsqcup \\bigsqcup \\times \\bigsqcup$ to make the largest product possible."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.353,
            0.847,
            0.393
          ],
          "content": "2. Fill the digits 3, 5, and 9 in $\\bigsqcup \\bigsqcup \\times \\bigsqcup$ to make the largest product possible."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.422,
            0.584,
            0.447
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "6.6 Decoding Divisibility Tricks"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.456,
            0.773,
            0.475
          ],
          "content": "It is now Mukta’s turn to show a mathematics trick to Shubham."
        },
